 "Erik  Piper" <erik@sky.cz> wrote:
> Really, there are two fundamentally different kinds of challenges in
> roguelikes that often unjustly get mentioned alongside each other:
> those that reward long, systematic preparation, the "persistence"
> mentioned in a post below -- a grind -- and those that reward choosing
> repeatedly the right tactics and (non-grind-based) strategy, where the
> range of these that enable these is narrow enough to be tough and broad
> enough not to be the same each time. A very hard type of challenge to
> devise, but rewarding for the player indeed. I will go out on a limb
> here and call the latter type "better."

I think that the ability to grind out success in NetHack is not a flaw.  
It allows for surprising and interesting challenges such as 
extinctionism.  And even then, failure is always an option: Witness the 
story of poor DeathOnAStick.

I would like to see a scoring system which rewards speed rather than 
persistence, but that's a minor issue.

What's important is that grinding is not required.  You don't need 1000 
hit points, every spell book, and an ocean of holy water to ascend.  
This makes the decision to grind a personal choice; every player can 
decide what balance of risk vs. speed they are willing to accept.

